[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. The reports merged into it
show more than one problem. They were merged on subject line alone
([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), so nobody read the bodies, and
different bugs that people describe the same way ended up in one ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: `parser` fixed, `hint` won't fix (booking-v3 retired).
`one-day` was in neither and is still open.
</Callout>

## Where each one stands

| problem | state | why |
|---|---|---|
| `parser` | fixed, closed | fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales |
| `hint` | won't fix, closed | the booking-v3 form is retired, so its hint is gone with it |
| `one-day` | **open** | no fix; not part of the engineering update on #4471 |

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

Every number here is computed from the export when the page renders (`#lib/kb/problems.ts`), so it
moves when the desk merges more reports.

### `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

Day-first tenants write 7/4 for 7 April; the parser stores 4 July. This is what #4471 describes,
and it is most of the reports. Every channel, every tenant.

### `hint`: booking-v3 tells people to type the month first

The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first tenants type what
it says, and the form reads their input day first. The date comes out swapped although the person
did what they were told. Only the booking form, only tenants on booking-v3. A parser fix leaves
this as it is; the hint has to change. Retiring booking-v3 removes it.

### `one-day`: dates move by one day

These are not swaps. Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May; one meeting reached Lisbon as Tuesday
and Toronto as Monday. No month/day mix-up moves a date by one day. It looks like a time-zone or
daylight-saving conversion bug. The desk already listed these reports in `desk-notes.json`; the
#4471 page still counts them under `parser`, where they have no ticket.

## How to tell them apart

1. Read the body, not the subject. Every subject in #4471 says "wrong day".
2. Day and month swapped, and the report came through a booking-v3 form: `hint`.
3. Day and month swapped, any other way in: `parser`.
4. Moved by one day: `one-day`.

Intake's tag (`meta.intakeTag`) guesses from the reporter's browser, not the tenant. Do not sort by it.

